    "result_text": "Confirmed \u2014 `HouseholdDuplicateException` is never thrown, so the separate household-duplicate 409 is inert, consistent with the spec.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the identity key and duplicate/soft-match logic to match the specification. All 5 tests pass (including the two acceptance tests that were previously failing).\n\n### Changes\n\n**`util/Soundex.java`** (new) \u2014 American Soundex encoder (`first letter + 3 digits`), with the standard H/W-transparent, vowel-resetting rules. Single responsibility: phonetic surname coding.\n\n**`util/Sha256Hex.java`** \u2014 added `lowerHex(String)` returning the full 64-char lower-case SHA-256 digest, reusing the existing private `sha256` helper (the old `upperHexPrefix` for customer code / household id is untouched).\n\n**`util/IdentityKey.java`** \u2014 `of(owner)` now returns `Sha256Hex.lowerHex(normalizedTelephone + \"|\" + lowerEmail + \"|\" + Soundex.encode(lastName))`. The household id is no longer part of identity. Email is lower-cased defensively; telephone is used in its stored E.164 form.\n\n**`rest/validation/PossibleDuplicateDetector.java`** \u2014 soft match now triggers when the **identity key differs** but **Soundex(lastName)** and **postcode** match (previously: same last name, same postcode, *different telephone*). It still returns the lowest-id match and skips when postcode is absent.\n\n**Docs** \u2014 updated the `identityKey` OpenAPI schema description/example, plus the javadocs on `IdentityKey`, `IdentityDuplicateValidator`, and `PossibleDuplicateDetector` to describe the new key.\n\n### Behaviour delivered\n- **409 duplicate detection** uses the single SHA-256 identity key, still filtering out soft-deleted owners (`activeOwners`) and still applying the disposable-email-domain blocklist first (`addOwner` order unchanged). The telephone pre-filter in `rejectIfDuplicateIdentity` remains valid since an equal key implies an equal telephone.\n- **Same lastName + postcode, different telephone** \u2192 no longer a hard duplicate; created as a **soft match** (their keys differ but Soundex + postcode match).\n- **Household-duplicate 409** from the computed `householdId` is gone from identity \u2014 it was already never thrown, and household id no longer participates in the key, so duplicate detection is now the single identity key.\n\nI kept each piece in its own small unit (encoding in `Soundex`, hashing in `Sha256Hex`, key derivation in `IdentityKey`, soft-match rule in `PossibleDuplicateDetector`) rather than expanding the controller.",
